  {
    "title": "Why not lock everything?",
    "body": "**Two-phase commit** offers all-or-nothing across databases: a coordinator asks each participant to prepare and lock, then tells all to commit. But participants hold their locks until the decision arrives, across every round trip, and if the coordinator crashes after \"prepare\" they stay blocked. The card provider does not take part in such a protocol at all. So between services, teams use sagas: short local transactions, compensations, an outbox and idempotency keys.",
